.TH SHUTDOWN 1
.SH NAME
shutdown \- warn the users and halt the machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shutdown
[\ \fB\-h\fR\ ]
.B now
.br
.B shutdown
[\ \fB\-h\fR\ ]
.BI + minutes
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shutdown
warns everyone logged in, stops the processes and halts the machine, so
that it can be turned off. With
.BR now ,
it does so at once; with
.BI + minutes\fR,
after that many minutes, warning the users then, every ten minutes, at
each of the last five, and at the end.
.B \-h
(halt) is taken, as 4.2BSD's shutdown takes it, but an XT can do
nothing else, so it is also what happens without it:
.PP
.nf
	# shutdown \-h now
	The system is going down NOW
.fi
.PP
At the end it sends every process the signal to end (SIGTERM), waits
five seconds for them, unmounts every file system but the root, writes
everything waiting to the disks, and runs XENIX's /etc/haltsys, which
halts the machine.
.PP
Only root may. Another account can, if it may use
.BR sudo (1):
.BR "sudo shutdown \-h now" .
XENIX's own shutdown is still /etc/shutdown.
.SH FILES
.TP 20
/etc/utmp
who is logged in, and on which terminal
.TP
/etc/mnttab
the mounted file systems
.TP
/etc/haltsys
XENIX's, which halts the machine
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"only root may halt the system" when run by another account. The exit
status is 2 after a mistake in the arguments; otherwise
.B shutdown
does not come back.
.SH SEE ALSO
sudo(1), sync(1), umount(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B shutdown
command appeared in 4.2BSD.
.SH AUTHORS
This shutdown was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
There is no
.BR \-r ,
to start the machine again: XENIX on an XT cannot.
